<div class="security-page">

    <h1>Security model: privacy by architecture</h1>

    <h2>Our approach</h2>
    <p class="security-lead">
        Most chat platforms ask you to trust their promises. Pisscord is built so that you don't have to trust us:
        your messages are encrypted on your device, your history stays on your device, and the servers in between only pass along
        data they cannot read.
    </p>

    <h2>Four principles</h2>
    <div class="security-principles">
        <div class="security-principle">
            <h3>1. We can't read your messages</h3>
            <p>Messages are end-to-end encrypted. They are encrypted on your device and decrypted only on the devices of the people you're talking to. Our servers never hold the keys.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="security-principle">
            <h3>2. There's nothing to hand over</h3>
            <p>Our central server is a relay, not an archive. It holds encrypted messages only for as long as it takes to deliver them. Your conversation history lives on your own devices, so there is no stored message history on our side for a breach, a data sale or a legal demand to reach.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="security-principle">
            <h3>3. No single point of failure</h3>
            <p>Anyone can host a community server. Communities don't have to live on infrastructure we control, and no company sits in the middle of every conversation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="security-principle">
            <h3>4. Security that doesn't get in the way</h3>
            <p>If privacy is hard to use, people go back to platforms that don't protect them. We design the interface so the secure option is the default and the only option.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>How it works</h2>

    <h3>End-to-end encryption</h3>
    <p>
        Every direct message and group chat is encrypted before it leaves your device.
        <mark>[Confirm protocol, e.g. the Signal Protocol for direct messages and MLS for group chats.]</mark>
        Voice and video calls are encrypted end to end as well. <mark>[Confirm scope for calls.]</mark>
    </p>

    <h3>Relay-only central server</h3>
    <p>
        Our central server's job is to deliver encrypted messages to the right place. When the recipient is offline, it holds the
        encrypted message temporarily and deletes it once it is delivered.
        <mark>[Confirm retention limit, e.g. deleted on delivery, or after a maximum of X days if undelivered.]</mark>
        Because messages are encrypted before they reach us, what we hold is unreadable.
    </p>

    <h3>Local-first storage</h3>
    <p>Your message history is stored on your own devices. If you use more than one device, they sync with each other using encrypted channels. We are not the backup of your conversations.</p>

    <h3>Community servers you can host yourself</h3>
    <p>Communities can run their own public servers. You choose who you trust with your community, and a compromise of one server does not expose everyone else on the platform.</p>

